Migrating your repository code from Manifest V2 to Manifest V3 introduces structural changes required by modern Chromium engines. [1] (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-edge/extensions/developer-guide/migrate-your-extension-from-manifest-v2-to-v3)
Here is the blueprint to update your extension configurations and scripts:

1. Update the Manifest File

Open your manifest.json file and make the following structural updates:
json
{
  // 1. Change version from 2 to 3
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Your Extension Name",
  "version": "1.0.0",

  // 2. Combine browser_action or page_action into 'action'
  "action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html",
    "default_icon": "icon.png"
  },

  // 3. Move background scripts to an event-driven service worker
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  },

  // 4. Split network / domain permissions from normal API permissions
  "permissions": [
    "storage",
    "tabs"
  ],
  "host_permissions": [
    "https://*://"
  ]
}
Use code with caution.

2. Refactor Background Code to a Service Worker

Manifest V3 replaces persistent background pages with ephemeral Service Workers. [1] (https://paulasantamaria.com/posts/chrome-extensions-migrating-to-manifest-v3-5e88/), [2] (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-edge/extensions/developer-guide/migrate-your-extension-from-manifest-v2-to-v3)
• No DOM Access: You cannot access window, document, or XMLHttpRequest directly in background.js. Replace network fetches with native fetch().
• State Persistence: Service workers stop running when idle. Instead of saving data to global variables, use the chrome.storage API to hold onto variables persistently.
• Listen to Events Directly: Register top-level listeners instead of embedding them inside structural window callbacks: [1] (https://css-tricks.com/how-to-transition-to-manifest-v3-for-chrome-extensions/), [2] (https://extensionbill.com/blog/migrate-chrome-extension-manifest-v3/)
javascript
// Inside background.js
chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(() => {
  console.log("Extension initialized successfully under Manifest V3!");
});
Use code with caution.

3. Core API Mapping Reassignments

Ensure that your scripts do not call legacy Manifest V2 APIs. Update your method calls according to this layout: [1] (https://stackoverflow.com/questions/71534432/migrate-chrome-extension-from-manifest-v2-to-v3-how-to-update-these-apis)

Old Manifest V2 API	New Manifest V3 Replacement
chrome.browserAction	chrome.action
chrome.pageAction	chrome.action
chrome.tabs.executeScript()	chrome.scripting.executeScript()
chrome.tabs.insertCSS()	chrome.scripting.insertCSS()

Note: Remotely hosted code blocks are completely banned in Manifest V3. All tracking or execution logic must be saved directly inside your extension package folder. [1] (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-edge/extensions/developer-guide/migrate-your-extension-from-manifest-v2-to-v3), [2] (https://extensionbill.com/blog/migrate-chrome-extension-manifest-v3/)
